<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!--
    Real generator consumer for the M9 per-view JsonTypeInfo phase (spec
    source-generator-json-typeinfo, task 8.1; Decision Log D125/D126). This is the JsonTypeInfo analogue
    of the Phase 2 a2n.Vista.StyleBExecSample and the Phase 4 a2n.Vista.GeneratorHttpSurfaceSample: it
    references BOTH Core and the EF layer AND the source generator (as an analyzer), so the
    ViewJsonContextGenerator (D125) emits — INTO this assembly, per COVERED view — a reflection-free
    <View>_VistaJsonContext.g.cs holding a System.Text.Json.Serialization.Metadata.IJsonTypeInfoResolver
    (built via JsonMetadataServices, NOT the [JsonSerializable] attribute route) that provides the
    JsonTypeInfo for the view's TRow, ViewListResult<TRow>, PagedResult<TRow>, and — when writable — TCrud,
    plus a [ModuleInitializer] that registers it into a2n.Vista.Core's GeneratedJsonContextStore keyed by
    the view's runtime Name.

    THE WHOLE POINT — NO DEVELOPER App_Json_Context. Unlike the Phase 4 HTTP-surface sample (which ships a
    developer-authored [JsonSerializable] App_Json_Context per view), this assembly declares NONE. The
    representative views are chosen so every DTO member is an Emittable_Shape, so the ViewJsonContextGenerator
    classifies each view as COVERED (VISTA0050) and emits its per-view JsonTypeInfo context — proving that a
    typed Style B app is AOT-clean for serialization WITHOUT any hand-authored context.

    COMPILE ONCE, QUANTIFY OVER VALUES: the master oracle-parity property test (task 8.2, Property 1) and the
    round-trip property test (task 8.3, Property 2) compile this fixture set ONCE, resolve each view's
    GENERATED context from the store, and compare its (de)serialization — over random DTO VALUES — against
    the reflection oracle. They never re-compile per iteration.

    Three representative, EF-aware typed Style B views are declared here (see JsonContextFixtures.cs):
      * a read-only SINGLE-KEY view (View<TRow>) whose TRow exercises scalar + nullable value type + enum +
        collection + byte[] members,
      * a read-only COMPOSITE-KEY view (View<TRow>, two-field key),
      * a WRITABLE view (View<TRow, TCrud>) whose TCrud is a record with init-only and required members,
        exercising the R2.5 parameterized/init construction paths.

    Multi-targeting (net8.0;net9.0;net10.0) is inherited from Directory.Build.props — do NOT override —
    so every generated context is proven to compile on every supported consumer TFM.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <RootNamespace>a2n.Vista.GeneratorJsonContextSample</RootNamespace>
    <AssemblyName>a2n.Vista.GeneratorJsonContextSample</AssemblyName>
    <IsPackable>false</IsPackable>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!-- The View<TQuery> / View<TQuery, TCrud> bases + the authoring DSL, ViewListResult<TRow>,
         PagedResult<TRow>, and the GeneratedJsonContextStore the module initializer targets all live in
         Core; the EF layer is referenced so the views are genuinely EF-aware (the compiled read/write path
         the generated context rides at runtime), mirroring the Phase 2 / Phase 4 fixtures. -->
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\a2n.Vista.Core\a2n.Vista.Core.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\a2n.Vista.EntityFrameworkCore\a2n.Vista.EntityFrameworkCore.csproj" />

    <!--
      Reference the generator as an ANALYZER only (no runtime assembly dependency) — exactly how a real
      consumer wires it. Because Core AND EF are referenced above, the ViewJsonContextGenerator recognizes
      the typed Style B views declared here and emits a real generated per-view JsonTypeInfo context +
      registration for each COVERED one (VISTA0050 is reported for each covered view).
    -->
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\a2n.Vista.SourceGenerators\a2n.Vista.SourceGenerators.csproj"
                      OutputItemType="Analyzer"
                      ReferenceOutputAssembly="false" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
